# Environment variables this app needs. Copy to `.env` and fill in.
#
# This file is the template, not the configuration: it holds no working values,
# and `.env` — which does — is excluded from git and from the Vercel upload
# (see .vercelignore). Nothing here is a secret; the values you supply are.

NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3000

# --- Application ---
# Any string identifying this deployment. Inlined into the browser bundle, so it
# is not a secret.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ID=

# --- Database ---
# REQUIRED. A PostgreSQL server reachable over TCP. This project uses Supabase.
#
#   postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres
#
# Supabase issues two connection strings per project, and the difference is not
# cosmetic:
#
#   Port 6543, session pooler - what the running application wants. PgBouncer
#     holds the connection count down, which matters when every serverless
#     invocation would otherwise open its own.
#   Port 5432, direct         - what `pnpm db:push` needs. DDL runs in a
#     transaction, and the transaction pooler hands the backend back after each
#     one, so a migration sent through 6543 fails partway through.
#
# So: run the app against 6543, run migrations against 5432. Both strings are in
# the Supabase dashboard under Connection settings, and they differ only in the
# port and hostname. `<project-ref>` is in both.
#
# The DDL in drizzle/ must be applied before the app serves any data:
#   DATABASE_URL=<the 5432 string> pnpm db:push
#
# Supabase's TLS certificate chains to a public CA, so DATABASE_SSL=require
# below is all the TLS this needs.
#
# The app needs this over TCP, and a network that blocks outbound Postgres
# cannot be worked around: there is no local database to point it at. HTTPS is
# not enough, because Supabase Auth is on 443 while the database is not — which
# is why a blocked network produces a sign-in that succeeds and then refuses.
DATABASE_URL=

# --- Database TLS ---
# Supabase refuses a connection that does not offer TLS. Its certificates chain
# to a public CA, so `require` verifies against the host trust store and needs no
# downloaded file. Both the app and `pnpm db:push` read this; it is the single
# place TLS is decided (server/_core/databaseConnection.ts).
#
# Only needed if you verify against Supabase's own CA certificate instead —
# then set DATABASE_CA_CERT and leave DATABASE_SSL unset:
#
#   DATABASE_CA_CERT=-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\nMIIB...
#   DATABASE_CA_CERT=./database/supabase-ca.crt
#
# A CA certificate is public, so it is safe in git and safe in a deployment's
# environment. The app logs which of the two is in force on its first connection;
# "TLS OFF" there means this variable is unset, which is wrong for Supabase.
#
# The GitHub Actions migration job DOES need this one. See the note on the
# workflow in README.md: the runner connects through the pooler, whose
# certificate is signed by Supabase's own CA rather than a public one.
DATABASE_SSL=require
DATABASE_CA_CERT=

# --- Database pool mode ---
# Only read when DATABASE_URL carries no port. `session` selects Supabase's
# session pooler (6544) instead of the default direct port, for the rare case
# that needs a held session. Leave unset for normal development and deployment;
# the URL's own port always wins.
DATABASE_POOL_MODE=

# --- Supabase Auth ---
# REQUIRED. Supabase owns credentials and sessions. This deployment holds no
# signing key of its own: it does not mint, verify or refresh a JWT, and it has
# no `JWT_SECRET`. Sign-in happens against Supabase from the browser, and the
# session cookie Supabase writes is what the server reads back on each request.
#
# Four variables, two values. The duplication is not an oversight and both pairs
# have to be right:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL         inlined into the browser bundle at BUILD
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY    time. This is what the sign-in form talks to.
#   SUPABASE_URL                     read by the server at RUN time.
#   SUPABASE_ANON_KEY
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY        server only. Never give this a NEXT_PUBLIC_
#                                    prefix: that would inline a key which
#                                    bypasses row level security into a bundle
#                                    anyone can download.
#
# Both pairs must name the SAME Supabase project. If the browser was built for
# one project and the server reads another, sign-in fails with "invalid login
# credentials" for a password that is correct, and nothing in the symptom points
# at the build. The server checks for that disagreement and refuses to start
# rather than let it out.
#
# If your host cannot separate build-time from run-time environment (most cannot),
# setting only the `NEXT_PUBLIC_` pair is enough — the server falls back to it.
#
# All three are in the Supabase dashboard under Project Settings -> API. The
# anon key is public by design and is in every browser; row level security is
# what constrains it, which is why migration 0001 turns RLS on and creates no
# policies. The service role key is the secret one: Project Settings -> API ->
# Service Role, and it bypasses RLS entirely. It is used for exactly three things
# — creating an account, generating a password-reset link, and setting a password
# directly — and every one of them is an administrator action.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- Object storage (case files and photographs) ---
# The Manus Forge API. Production fails loudly without these rather than
# silently writing uploads to its own filesystem, where they would be lost on the
# next deploy.
BUILT_IN_FORGE_API_URL=
BUILT_IN_FORGE_API_KEY=

# --- Bootstrapping the first account ---
# An empty database has nobody in it, and nothing in the application can create
# the first user: the admin screen that creates accounts is behind a capability
# only an administrator already holds. So the first account is made from
# outside, once, by `pnpm db:seed`.
#
# Run it with the same DATABASE_URL the deployment uses:
#
#   DATABASE_URL=... SUPABASE_URL=... SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=... \
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=... SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=... pnpm db:seed
#
# It creates a single super_admin, and re-running it changes nothing unless
# SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD is offered again — which is how a lost password is
# replaced. The password is checked against the same rule the admin screen
# enforces, and is never invented for you.
#
# SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL matters more than it looks: it is where Supabase sends a
# password reset. Seeded at the `example.com` default, the one account that can
# unlock the platform cannot be recovered by the only documented route. In
# production the seed refuses to run at that address at all.
#
# Not a runtime variable: the application never reads any of these. Unset them
# once the first account exists.
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=
SEED_ADMIN_NAME=
SEED_ADMIN_USERNAME=
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# --- Demonstration data ---
# Optional. `database/test-data.sql` fills the register with invented matters so
# the dashboard, the reports and the oversight list have something in them. It is
# a file rather than a script because it is fixture data a person should read
# before it runs:
#
#   pnpm db:testdata
#
# Requires a super administrator (so run `pnpm db:seed` first), is additive and
# idempotent, and invents every name in it. Use the direct connection (5432) for
# the same reason migrations do.

# --- Moving the pre-PostgreSQL data across ---
# The platform's data predates PostgreSQL: it was exported from MySQL into
# database/tsc_case_management_dump.sql, which is the only copy of it and is
# deliberately not in git (see .gitignore — it holds password hashes and
# personal data).
#
# To bring it into whichever database DATABASE_URL currently names:
#
#   1. Put the *direct* connection string in DATABASE_URL (Supabase 5432, not
#      the pooler) — DDL and bulk inserts both need a real session.
#   2. Create the schema:   pnpm db:push
#   3. Load the data:       pnpm db:load
#
# `db:load` is `db:convert` (which rewrites the MySQL dump as PostgreSQL INSERTs
# into database/postgres-load.sql) followed by psql against DATABASE_URL. It runs
# as one transaction, so a failure partway leaves the database empty rather than
# half populated.
#
# The conversion handles three things that a search-and-replace gets wrong: MySQL
# string escaping, tinyint(1) booleans, and advancing each serial sequence past
# the ids it loads. That last one matters most — without it the import appears to
# succeed and the first write afterwards fails on a duplicate key.
#
# Note that the imported rows carry no `authUserId`: they predate Supabase Auth.
# An account with a null `authUserId` cannot sign in, and the admin screen shows
# it as unprovisioned. Provision the real officers from there, which is also the
# only way their Supabase identity gets created.
#
# Only needed once, when moving between database servers. It is not a runtime
# step, and re-running it against a populated database will fail on the primary
# keys rather than duplicate rows.
